 total_revenue = orders.sum { |order| order.amount }</code></pre><h2>ActiveRecord Relations as Enumerables</h2><p>One of the most powerful features of Ruby on Rails is that <code>ActiveRecord::Relation</code> objects include the <code>Enumerable</code> module. This means you can chain SQL queries and immediately apply Ruby enumerable methods. However, with great power comes great responsibility.</p><p>Consider the difference between filtering in the database versus filtering in memory:</p><div class="table-container"><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Method Example</th><th>Execution Point</th><th>Performance Impact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Database Level (Recommended)</td><td><code>User.where(active: true)</code></td><td>Executed in SQL via database engine</td><td>High performance; leverages DB indexes and limits data transfer.</td></tr><tr><td>Memory Level (Enumerable)</td><td><code>User.all.select(&amp;:active)</code></td><td>Loaded into Ruby memory, then filtered</td><td>Low performance for large datasets; loads entire table into RAM.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>As a best practice in Rails development, always push filtering and data manipulation as far down into the database layer as possible using ActiveRecord querying methods before relying on Ruby enumerables for final transformation.</p><h2>Lazy Evaluation with .lazy</h2><p>When working with infinite sequences or massive datasets where you only need the first few results, standard enumerables can be bottlenecks because they evaluate eagerly. Introduced in Ruby 2.0, the <code>lazy</code> method allows you to perform lazy evaluation, processing elements on-demand:</p><pre><code># Processes only as many elements as necessary
 first_three = (1..Float::INFINITY).lazy.map { |n| n * 2 }.first(3)</code></pre><h2>Step-by-Step Checklist for Using Enumerables in Rails</h2><ol><li><strong>Identify the Data Source:</strong> Determine whether you are working with an in-memory array/hash or an ActiveRecord relation.</li><li><strong>Optimize Queries First:</strong> Use ActiveRecord scopes and database queries (<code>where</code>, <code>pluck</code>, <code>select</code>) to minimize dataset size.</li><li><strong>Choose the Right Enumerable:</strong> Use <code>map</code> for transformation, <code>select</code> for filtering, <code>detect</code> for searching, and <code>reduce</code> for aggregation.</li><li><strong>Leverage Shorthands:</strong> Use symbol-to-proc syntax (<code>&amp;:method_name</code>) to keep your blocks concise and readable.</li><li><strong>Avoid N+1 Queries:</strong> Ensure collections involving associations are properly eager-loaded using <code>includes</code> or <code>joins</code> before iterating.</li></ol><h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2><h3>What is an Enumerable in Ruby on Rails?</h3><p>An Enumerable is a module in Ruby that provides a set of traversal, searching, sorting, and filtering methods to collection classes like Arrays, Hashes, and ActiveRecord relations.</p><h3>What is the difference between map and select in Ruby?</h3><p>The <code>map</code> method transforms each element in a collection and returns a new collection of the same size. The <code>select</code> method filters the collection, returning only the elements that evaluate to true for the given block condition.</p><h3>Can ActiveRecord relations use Ruby enumerable methods?</h3><p>Yes. <code>ActiveRecord::Relation</code> includes the <code>Enumerable</code> module, allowing you to use methods like <code>map</code>, <code>select</code>, and <code>reject</code> directly on database query results.</p><h3>Why should I prefer database queries over Ruby enumerable filtering?</h3><p>Filtering via database queries (using ActiveRecord) executes logic inside the database engine, which is optimized for indexing and large datasets, whereas Ruby enumerable filtering loads all records into server memory first.</p>
 <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What are collections in Ruby?</h3><p>Collections group data — mainly arrays (ordered lists) and hashes (key-value pairs). They're the core structures you use to hold and organize data in Ruby.</p><h3>What is Enumerable in Ruby?</h3><p>A module that adds powerful iteration methods — like map, select, and reduce — to collections, letting you transform and filter data cleanly without manual loops.</p><h3>What's the difference between map and each?</h3><p>each runs a block for every item (for side effects), while map runs a block and returns a new array of the results — use map when you want a transformed collection back.</p><h3>What does reduce do in Ruby?</h3><p>reduce (also inject) combines all items into a single value — for example summing a list or building a total — by applying a block cumulatively.</p><h3>Why are Enumerable methods important?</h3><p>They let you express data transformations in one readable line, which is central to idiomatic Ruby and to working effectively with Rails' data.</p><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@graph": [{"@type": "BlogPosting", "headline": "Ruby Collections & Enumerables Explained (2026)", "about": "ruby collections and enumerables", "url": "https://studenttimepk.blogspot.com/2025/12/lesson-2-collections-enumerables-logi.html", "dateModified": "2026-09-15", "author": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Students Time"}, "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Students Time"}}, {"@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "What are collections in Ruby?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Collections group data \u2014 mainly arrays (ordered lists) and hashes (key-value pairs). They're the core structures you use to hold and organize data in Ruby."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is Enumerable in Ruby?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "A module that adds powerful iteration methods \u2014 like map, select, and reduce \u2014 to collections, letting you transform and filter data cleanly without manual loops."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What's the difference between map and each?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "each runs a block for every item (for side effects), while map runs a block and returns a new array of the results \u2014 use map when you want a transformed collection back."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What does reduce do in Ruby?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "reduce (also inject) combines all items into a single value \u2014 for example summing a list or building a total \u2014 by applying a block cumulatively."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Why are Enumerable methods important?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "They let you express data transformations in one readable line, which is central to idiomatic Ruby and to working effectively with Rails' data."}}]}]}</script><!--related-guides-->
